<!DOCTYPE html>
<%# THE PUBLIC LAYOUT IS THE CMS'S AND NO THEME REPLACES IT (theming spec §2,
    decision 10). What a site breaks without is written here; what is look or
    voice is one of four partials a theme replaces, each with the default
    theme's as the fallback:

      layouts/public/_title   the pattern of the page title
      layouts/public/_icons   the favicon and the touch icon
      layouts/public/_head    the theme's own tags (fonts, theme-colour)
      layouts/public/_frame   everything inside <body> but the preview bar

    The CMS's tags, which a theme cannot remove: the <title> element, the
    viewport, the CSP tag, the script that picks light or dark before the
    first paint, the description and Open Graph tags (pages/_seo_meta), the
    canonical and language links, the stylesheet link and the import map.
    `test/integration/theme_pages_test.rb` ("every tag the CMS writes into
    the head is there exactly once under both themes, ahead of the theme's
    own") holds the list. A theme's `_head` can print more markup, a tag of
    the same name included: the CMS's own are printed once and first, and
    refusing such a `_head` is the folder check's. %>
<html lang="<%= I18n.locale %>"<%= " class=\"scroll-pt-9\"".html_safe if @preview_record %>>
  <head>
    <%# Whether the response describes a record, for the title here and the
        description and Open Graph tags below.

        Not for a 404, and the response's status is what says so, not
        `@page`: a 404 can have a record in hand (`assign_record` assigns
        the page and the post before it finds that no block lists the post,
        and the preview answers 404 for a post whose listing page the editor
        may not read), and its title and description are not a 404's to
        print. While pages/show rendered the tags, not rendering pages/show
        was the guard
        (`test/integration/theme_pages_test.rb`: "a 404 that had a record in
        hand describes none"). %>
    <% described = page_found? %>
    <%# The title's text is asked for here and not read from a
        `content_for(:title)` slot: `content_for` appends, so a theme's
        template that wrote to the slot would have its text put in front of
        the page's ("HijackAbout – Site"). The pattern is the theme's
        (layouts/public/_title); the element and what goes into it are not
        (`test/integration/theme_pages_test.rb`: "a theme's template that
        writes to the title's slot changes no title"). %>
    <title><%= described ? document_title(@page, post: @post) : themed_title(page_title: nil, site_title: Locales.read(Current.site, :title)) %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <%# No csrf_meta_tags (spec §6.4): PublicController serves the same
        cookie-less response to every visitor, and Rails only reads/writes
        the session -- which would set a cookie on an otherwise anonymous,
        publicly cacheable page -- to mint the token these tags embed. %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>
    <%= render "shared/theme_head" %>

    <%# The description and the Open Graph tags, printed here by the layout
        and not read from a `content_for(:head)` slot, for the reason the
        title is not: the page is rendered before the layout, `content_for`
        appends, and a theme's page or block template that wrote to the slot
        would have its tags printed here, among the CMS's own and ahead of
        the description. The theme's part of the head is
        layouts/public/_head, below
        (`test/integration/theme_pages_test.rb`: "a theme's template that
        writes to the head's slot puts nothing into the head"; the folder
        check refuses a template that writes to the slot, `Theme::Check`). %>
    <%= render "pages/seo_meta", page: @page, post: @post if described %>

    <%# hreflang alternates (spec §6.5): every enabled language's URL for the
        page or post currently shown, plus x-default pointing search engines
        at the site's own default language rather than making them guess.

        Not for a 404, by the response's status and not by the record, for
        the reason above: a 404 can have a page and a post in hand, and a
        canonical link or an alternate on it would name the missing page as
        the record's own address in every language
        (`test/integration/theme_pages_test.rb`: "a 404 that had a record in
        hand names no canonical address and no other language's, in the
        preview and in public"). %>
    <% if page_found? && (alternate_record = @post || @page) %>
      <%# The one address of what is shown: the record's own in this language,
          on the site's configured address, with no query string -- so
          `/about?utm=1` and a request under any other `Host` name the same
          page. Not in a preview (its addresses are not the public's). %>
      <% if !public_urls.preview? && (canonical_url = public_urls.url_for(alternate_record)) %>
        <link rel="canonical" href="<%= canonical_url %>">
      <% end %>
      <% public_urls.alternates(alternate_record).each do |code, url| %>
        <link rel="alternate" hreflang="<%= code %>" href="<%= url %>">
      <% end %>
      <% if (default_url = public_urls.url_for(alternate_record, Locales.default)) %>
        <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="<%= default_url %>">
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <%= render "layouts/public/icons" %>
    <%= render "layouts/public/head" %>

    <%# The one public stylesheet (theming spec §4): the default theme's
        styles and then the theme's, built together (`ThemeCss`) -- never the
        product stylesheet. `site` is the public site's entry point:
        `application` plus the theme's controllers (app/javascript/site.js). %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "theme", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags "site" %>
  </head>

  <%# In a preview the bar (layouts/public/_preview_banner) is fixed to the
      top of the window and out of the flow, so the body is pushed down by its
      height (`pt-9`, 36px) and anchor jumps keep clear of it (`scroll-pt-9` on
      <html>). Neither exists on the public site proper. The bar is product UI
      on a public page: it is rendered here, by the CMS, and no theme
      replaces it.

      `overflow-x-clip` is for a full-width section (sections spec §5.3,
      `themes/default/sections.css`): its band is `100vw` wide, and `100vw`
      includes a classic scrollbar's width, so without the clip every page
      with such a band would scroll sideways by those few pixels. On <body>
      the value is handed to the viewport, which stays the page's one
      scroller -- so a `position: sticky` element and a code block's own
      sideways scroll (`.cs-prose pre`) behave as before.

      The classes on <body> are the default theme's and read tokens only: a
      theme changes the page's ground and type by its tokens, or by a `body`
      rule of its own, which comes later in the one stylesheet. %>
  <body class="min-h-screen overflow-x-clip bg-paper font-cs text-ink scheme-light dark:scheme-dark<%= " pt-9" if @preview_record %>">
    <%= render "layouts/public/preview_banner" if @preview_record %>
    <%= render layout: "layouts/public/frame" do %><%= yield %><% end %>
  </body>
</html>
